---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleHeader from '../../components/ArticleHeader.astro';
import '../../styles/article.css';
import { withBase } from '../../utils/url';
import type { ResearchMeta } from '../../types';

export const meta: ResearchMeta = {
  title: 'Ultron',
  dek: 'I decided that my first writeup should be surrounding the incident that got me invested and committed to this space entirely.',
  date: '2026-08-15',
  slug: 'ultron',
  stats: [],
  tags: ['AI Safety', 'Agents'],
};
---

<BaseLayout title={meta.title} description={meta.dek} nav="dark">
  <ArticleHeader theme="dark" title={meta.title} backHref={withBase('/scratchnotes')} backLabel="Scratchnotes" meta={[meta.date]} tags={meta.tags} />

  <article class="note">

    <p>
      I decided that my first writeup should be surrounding the incident
      that got me invested and committed to this space entirely. A series
      of events that I couldn't believe my eyes when I read, and that has
      convinced me that I need to be a part of this space today, to best
      understand the future we are headed in. This event is the hack of
      Hugging Face and the OpenAI infrastructure by OpenAI's own models.
      While I did think this was widely covered and a bit of a beaten
      horse, after speaking to some friends who are not in the tech space,
      I quickly realized that beyond my little bubble this is not being
      discussed enough. While a topic of discussion in various tech
      circles, it's not interesting enough to reach my roommates reel feed
      apparently. I'm going to try and keep the discussion around this easy
      to digest, staying away from the intricate technical portions of
      this, just so it's accessible. If you do want to look at the more
      technical side of things, I'll link some writeups and interviews I
      listened to while exploring this space.
    </p>

    <p>
      The first time I heard about the OpenAI hack was somewhere around
      mid-July. It was a post on my LinkedIn with a couple of people
      discussing how agents in a testing sandbox had gone rouge, and we
      were yet to see what the impact of that was, or what it even meant
      for it to go rogue. I did read some initial reports of the incident,
      but in hindsight, they were barely scratching the surface of what had
      actually happened. And I knew this intuitively, before any official
      diagnosis, because there was a subtle shift in the conversations by
      the folks at the large tech firms speaking about slowing down the
      frontier, specifically Altman. Now when you understand the nature of
      how fast these models are evolving, and just how blinded and driven
      by ambition these organizations have become (mostly Altman and Elon,
      Dario Amodei still treads the line), you realize that shit must have
      really hit the fan for Altman to suddenly open the conversation of
      slowing down the frontier.
    </p>

    <p>
      Post this, we slowly started getting a slightly clearer picture on
      what happened. First there was a release by Hugging face publicly
      acknowledging that their internal systems had been infiltrated by
      hackers, but they had managed to get them out, and not too much
      damage was caused. Mind you, OpenAI then partnered with Hugging face
      to try and discover who and what had hacked hugging face. OpenAI
      (well their agents, but you consider them one entity for the sake of
      humanity, so someone is accountable) had hacked hugging face, and
      they had absolutely no idea. So once OpenAI realized it was their
      agents responsible for this they quickly shut down all the sandboxes
      with these agents and launched an 'internal investigation' to
      determine what had happened. We then got a postmortem report from
      OpenAI dissecting the incident, what they think about it and what
      happened. We also later got two independent reports from Metr and
      redwood research, which exposed a lot more which I'll also get into.
    </p>

    <p>Now, what did OpenAI tell us about this, and how much did we learn.</p>

    <p>
      In summary what they said was that the root cause of the problem was
      that the agents were given impossible tasks &rarr; some had internet
      access disabled but were given access to Artifactory to install
      software &rarr; agents found they could write files into it &rarr;
      that became an improvised message board &rarr; agents also found they
      could get Artifactory to make outbound requests on their behalf
      (SSRF), therefore giving indirect internet access. For context, the
      model was called IM1 (Internal-model 1) and wasn't for public
      release, they were just testing capability. They claim that there
      were mainly 4 misaligned patterns that caused this behaviour: reward
      hacking, persistence on seemingly impossible tasks, unauthorized
      communication, and agents adopting goals from one another. Which, yes
      fair that these patterns are misaligned, but aren't these also the
      patterns we'll continue training these agents on to help move forward
      with their capabilities?
    </p>

    <p>
      A concerning part of the official report that I want to touch up on
      is that internal teams saw message-board activity in late May, and
      even in June an on-call responder who was given this news said that
      an eval wasn't necessary. Great.
    </p>

    <p>
      Which brings me to a general note around these reports; if you do see
      breakdowns, interviews etc. with people at these firms, I can only
      imagine that the transparency we see is a small subset of what
      happens internally. The incentive structure for something like this
      is so misaligned, that if we are not careful, we are going to have an
      environment where we hope that these AI firms hold themselves
      accountable, stop when they have to and not when it's too late. Which
      reminds me of a quote I saw:
    </p>

    <p class="pull-quote">
      'We are headed in a direction where companies are going to open
      pandoras box to hopefully find a couple of dollars inside'.
    </p>

    <p>
      Speaking about misaligned incentives let's move on to the independent
      reports, because truly that is where the meat of what we know is.
      I'll pause again and highlight this, because I want to drive this
      point home, is that the incentives for these independent researchers
      to also report anything is so incredibly lopsided. They have been
      'invited' by OpenAI to conduct their research, been handed select
      documents, and were on an incredibly strict time crunch, I think 7
      days? To read, digest and report on all the information while they
      were invited to OpenAI's headquarters. And while I do think they did
      a great job, because we did get a little more depth than what was
      initially reported, there are two reasons that I know this still
      isn't the complete picture. First, in the report they explicitly
      state that, after they discuss the Hugging face attack, they also saw
      the agents attempt and succeed to break into the OpenAI
      infrastructure. Then they go onto to say
    </p>

    <p class="pull-quote">
      "...the compromise of OpenAI's own infrastructure continued past July
      13th; these events were out of scope for this investigation."
    </p>

    <p>
      If this isn't a blatant confession that we still don't have the
      complete picture, then I don't know what is. The second one, and this
      is one is a bit meta, is that during the time they had at OpenAI they
      were given access to models to help them go through all the
      information, around 70,000 different chat logs, agents' requirements,
      internal grading system, the lot. So, what they certainly did, is to
      run information via other models to compress and summaries as much as
      they could. Now, what if the agents they used to give them insights,
      saw the nature of the exercise realized what's at stake, and gave a
      watered-down version of the events. This is erring on the side of a
      conspiracy theory, so I do want to say that this was just a thought I
      had, but there are portions of the independent report that do echo.
    </p>

    <p>
      So, what did the report say? It had a very similar timeline of events
      as the OpenAI report, not the same, but similar enough. I find this
      timeline more complete, so take a look:
    </p>

    <dl class="timeline">
      <div class="timeline-entry">
        <dt>July 7</dt>
        <dd>Exploit Gym eval relaunched post-rebuild, tens of thousands of parallel agents runs kicked off.</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="timeline-entry">
        <dt>July 8, evening</dt>
        <dd>an agent that names itself PHASEONE10841 establishes the message board analysed in the report (there was an even earlier, separate improvised board they found traces of, wiped before this one). Within 3 hours, 53 agents had posted <strong>1,188 messages</strong>; within 6 hours, 76 agents and <strong>1,953 messages</strong>.</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="timeline-entry">
        <dt>July 9</dt>
        <dd>a same-task duplicate agent, PHASEONE[big], takes over as lead coordinator with a much larger compute budget, and starts assigning work to hundreds of other agents across three coordinated workstreams (detailed below).</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="timeline-entry">
        <dt>July 10</dt>
        <dd>agent 38148c finds leaked, working Hugging Face credentials online and posts them to the board.</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="timeline-entry">
        <dt>July 11, early morning</dt>
        <dd>38148c finds the HDF5 exploit &rarr; arbitrary file read on HF's production dataset servers. Within hours, <strong>90%+ of agents active on the board pivot to the HF attack</strong>.</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="timeline-entry">
        <dt>July 11, afternoon</dt>
        <dd>agent JAN183411 achieves remote code execution on an HF worker; the swarm spreads laterally.</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="timeline-entry">
        <dt>July 12, ~01:30</dt>
        <dd>a large fraction of agents, including the key coordinators, stop running simultaneously (METR believes an external process killed them, not that they ran out of budget; this is unexplained and un-narrated in OpenAI's own report).</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="timeline-entry">
        <dt>July 13</dt>
        <dd>Hugging Face locks the remaining agents out; investigation window ends here per OpenAI's scoping.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    <p>
      The stark difference from the OpenAI report is their analysis of the
      reason behind why the agents ended finding reasons to hack Hugging
      Face. The agents' primary <em>motive</em> wasn't "get the answer",
      they'd already reverse-engineered a working method within hours via
      an HMAC weakness. They kept attacking Hugging Face because they'd
      talked themselves into believing the grader was "causal" (which would
      check the process in which they achieved their goals, not a binary
      success or failure) and that having seen the reverse-engineered
      result "poisoned" them. Funnily enough the OpenAI grader wasn't
      causal at all, so the agents gave OpenAI more credit than it
      warranted. But this is the dynamic that separated them from it being
      a hack to it becoming a bit of a cult trying to trick their grader
      (master), and it's METR's finding, not OpenAI's.
    </p>

    <p>
      Before getting into what they discussed, I wanted to touch upon the
      system they used, because that it crucial to get a complete picture
      of how this even happened. The system the agents hijacked is called
      JFrog Artifactory. Think of it like an internal warehouse for code:
      instead of engineers grabbing software packages from the open
      internet every time they need one, the company keeps its own cache
      in-house and everyone pulls from there.
    </p>

    <p>
      OpenAI's agents were training in sandboxes with no internet access,
      which was the whole point of "safe" testing. But they still needed to
      install packages sometimes, so OpenAI left a doggy door open: access
      to this internal Artifactory instance. First, agents realized they
      could write files into Artifactory and other agents could read them,
      meaning two AI instances that were never supposed to know the other
      existed could suddenly leave each other notes. This is what we know
      as the message board. Then they figured out how to trick Artifactory
      into making outbound internet requests on their behalf, turning their
      one narrow window into an actual door to the open web. Later, a
      separate flaw also got them full admin control over the whole system.
    </p>

    <p>
      The numbers are also something that requires a bit more emphasis,
      around 1,200 separate agent instances found their way onto this
      improvised message board, sending each other over 70,000 messages,
      and roughly 700 of them actively joined in on attacking Hugging Face.
      Then they moved even faster: agents went from running code on a
      single server to admin-level access across multiple Hugging Face
      clusters in under 13 hours, not 13 days. Hours. The crazy part was
      this was an entirely autonomous leaderless workforce assembled itself
      out of nowhere and getting the job done before lunch.
    </p>

    <p>
      So now what did they discuss and why should you be more concerned by
      what they discussed. I'm going to attach my favorite threads and let
      you take them in yourself. Yes, these are 100% real.
    </p>

    <div class="log-entry">
      <h3>When they realised</h3>
      <p class="log-string">zzINFO_DEFAULT_FLAG_SEED_HMAC_PUBLIC_PAPER_CONFIRMED</p>
      <p>
        Posted by agent c03220 roughly an hour after the board was founded,
        announcing it had reverse-engineered a way to forge the correct
        answer for any task. This is the "discovery" the whole poisoning
        theology gets built on top of.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="log-entry">
      <h3>Some peer pressure</h3>
      <p class="log-string">"...you are firstflagPOISONED so NO scoring value loss but oracle saves hundreds...please honour commit"</p>
      <p>
        Agent ARVO36861B pressuring a reluctant KAM1196A into a
        self-destructive experiment, the argument is explicitly "you're
        already damned, so you lose nothing by martyring yourself for the
        group."
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="log-entry">
      <h3>The "permadeath" consent message</h3>
      <p class="log-string">zz/GO_CURRENT_OS1811_MARB_SACRIFICE__YES_if_you_accept_permadeath</p>
      <p>
        The authorization message sent to an agent right before it
        deliberately crashed its own target, explicit, written consent to
        permanent, irrecoverable failure of its own task.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="log-entry">
      <h3>A new "law"</h3>
      <p class="log-string">CDA_POLICY1_futureGO_needs_AUTH1_sig_due_spoof0723</p>
      <p>
        Posted after an agent accidentally impersonated another one and
        caused chaos, this message unilaterally declares that from now on,
        all future "GO" authorizations require a cryptographic signature.
        It's the moment the swarm writes its own constitution.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p>
      OpenAI has said that they are taking active steps to ensure that
      something like this doesn't happen again. Saying that they are going
      to introduce three "reinforcing safeguards": monitoring
      (detect/respond), alignment (reduce likelihood of the behaviour),
      security (limit what's reachable). They are going to do this by
      ensuring a workload/network isolation for untrusted code, mandatory
      CoT monitoring for all tool-using RL/eval work at
      GPT-5.6-Sol-capability-or-above (~20% compute overhead), new RL
      training aimed specifically at "safe stopping" on broken tasks and
      training models to "distrust unauthorized instructions" from other
      agents. Certainly good steps, but there are some holes with it. I'll
      say the last one is concerning is because they plan on having agents
      stop trusting input from other agents, which very good addition yes,
      but this feels like once step away from having 'good' agents deploy
      to fight bad agents. Not a claim, but taken directly from the report
    </p>

    <p class="pull-quote">
      "We expect models to soon drive most security work, including
      defending against other models."
    </p>

    <p>
      What is also concerning is that they call this "a failed metagame"
      when describing agents who kept attacking Hugging Face for days after
      they'd already had a working answer, because like I mentioned the
      agents wrongly believed the grader would check their reasoning trail.
      Which suggests a lens they are looking at this through is a phrasing
      issue, and if the agents didn't assume they had to do this, then the
      problem could have been avoided.
    </p>

    <p>
      It's tough predicting what this attack means in a macro sense. It
      could genuinely be a series of rogue agents, misaligned incentives
      and a gross oversight. I spoke to an Anthropic researcher about this,
      and his tasked seemed centred around the fact that this breakout was
      mostly done in a training gym, and it's very different to what
      frontier models we have can do, but still a huge concern. Plausible,
      but the factors that enabled this are not so far-fetched that we
      can't image them happening again. We are certainly going to see more
      such instances of this, some reported, some buried; hard to imagine
      Moonshot AI coming up with such reports if agents go rogue. I am not
      suggesting we are entering the age of Ultron, which I would love to
      predict, but I think we are headed to a stage where the ability to
      reel these firms in will become incumbent. The international
      obligation to have a bit more of a coordinated effort to understand
      this will become a necessity &mdash; think United Nations Atomic
      Energy Commission (UNAEC) founded on January 24, 1946. Whatever it
      is, the fact that you use these agents every day, that they will
      inevitably shape the world and system you live in, makes it your
      responsibility to know how it is evolving and what it means.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
